<!--
=============================================================================
ASHFALL OUTPOST - single-file turn-based survival management game
=============================================================================

ASSUMPTIONS (made without asking, as instructed)
-----------------------------------------------
1.  "Resources" are the six headline resources (food, water, metal, power,
    parts, scrip) plus three intermediate goods (ash, ore, soil) that exist
    only to make the production chains real. All nine are stored and capped
    (scrip is uncapped - it is money, not cargo).
2.  Power is a stored resource with a small cap, not an instantaneous flow.
    Unused power stays in the batteries up to the cap; overflow is lost.
3.  A "turn" is roughly a week. Consumption is 0.9 food and 1.1 water per
    colonist per turn.
4.  Adjacency bonuses use the 4-neighbourhood (orthogonal), and they multiply
    a building's OUTPUT only - inputs scale with worker rate alone. That makes
    good placement a genuine efficiency gain.
5.  Building condition (hp) scales output linearly from 40%..100% between
    hp 25 and hp 100. Below hp 25 the building is OFFLINE and produces nothing
    until repaired. Repairs cost parts AND labour (an idle colonist, who gains
    fatigue and loses that turn's rest).
6.  Colonists never die from hunger. Shortages crush morale; a colonist at
    morale 0 refuses to work, and if they stay at 0 for two consecutive turns
    they walk out of the gate. Every consequence is recoverable.
7.  Population growth is immigration, not birth: newcomers arrive when there
    is spare housing, healthy morale and a visible food/water surplus.
8.  Research is bought with scrip and parts (no research-point currency).
    A staffed Lab discounts costs; the Salvaged Archives node discounts them
    further.
9.  Ashstorms hit on every 6th turn. Their grit hangs in the air, so the water
    surcharge lands on the two turns AFTER the storm, not the storm turn
    itself (the storm resolves after that turn's consumption step).
10. Win is evaluated at the end of turn 60. Reaching turn 60 without meeting
    the bar is not a loss, it is a lesser ending ("Endured") - only population
    zero is a true loss. Both show the scored summary.
11. The Dev benchmark runs 200 turns in a sandbox that ignores the turn-60
    ending so the full 200 turns always resolve; its greedy policy is pure
    (reads state, never calls the PRNG) so the hash is reproducible.
12. No storage of any kind is used. Save/load is copy-paste JSON only.

TURN RESOLUTION ORDER (fixed, and printed in the Log tab)
---------------------------------------------------------
  1. Validate assignments (orphaned / over-capacity workers are freed)
  2. Production: power -> raw -> intermediate -> finished -> services
  3. Storage caps applied; overflow logged
  4. Consumption (food, water, + lingering ashfall water surcharge)
  5. Needs resolve into morale
  6. Clinic care, then fatigue (work adds, rest removes)
  7. Skill growth from work
  8. Departures (morale 0 for two turns running)
  9. Arrivals (immigration check)
 10. Ashstorm check (every 6th turn)
 11. Event deck (weighted, with cooldowns)
 12. Caravan movement and market drift
 13. Flag / cooldown decay
 14. Win / loss evaluation
 15. Turn counter increments

All randomness comes from one seeded mulberry32 stream stored in the save.
Math.random is never called anywhere in this file.
=============================================================================
-->
